                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the failed pilot`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `The pilot looked fine and changed nothing: 82% completion, 3.9/5 enjoyment — yet only <strong>11% of staff</strong> changed their customer conversations, perceived usefulness was 2.4/5 and trainer credibility 2.6/5. Leadership measured the wrong thing. Using Giangreco's research, separate <em>satisfaction (did they enjoy it?)</em> from the drivers that actually predict use — <strong>perceived usefulness, trainer performance and efficiency</strong>. Name the two failure drivers and the metric leadership should have watched. The trap: deciding completion or enjoyment was too low and simply doing more of it.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Set content relevance (design for usefulness)`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Perceived usefulness is the strongest driver of transfer — so design the content for job relevance. Choose how far to <strong>tailor to real Aurore customer scenarios</strong> and the bank's own products versus buying a cheap generic off-the-shelf course, whether to segment by branch type and seniority, and how much to invest in practice on real products. Every choice costs against the <em>€792-per-learner cap</em>. The trap: economizing on relevance — a generic course scores 2.4/5 on usefulness and gets ignored on the job, exactly like the pilot.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Choose trainer, format & reinforcement`,
