                    coach_r1_cause_right:   `<strong>That's the right read.</strong> The pilot failed on perceived usefulness (2.4/5) and trainer credibility (2.6/5) — the drivers Giangreco's research links to use — not on enjoyment, which was fine at 3.9/5. Fix the drivers that actually move behaviour.`,
                    coach_r1_enjoyment:     `<strong>Enjoyment was the one thing that worked.</strong> The happy sheets averaged 3.9/5 and the training still changed nothing. Making it <em>more</em> fun targets the metric that already looked fine. Which choice raises usefulness, not enjoyment?`,
                    coach_r1_completion:    `<strong>Coverage was never the problem.</strong> 82% completed and 89% changed nothing. Pushing attendance harder gives you a higher completion number and the same 11% transfer. Aurore has a transfer problem, not a coverage problem.`,
                    coach_r1_staff:         `<strong>Blaming the staff is the comfortable answer.</strong> When 89% of 2,400 people don't apply a course, that's a design and reinforcement problem, not careless people. They rated it 2.4/5 useful — they're telling you why.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named usefulness and credibility as the failed drivers AND said leadership should have watched transfer and usefulness, not the happy-sheet. That's the distinction at the heart of the research — now design against it.`,
